SQLite benchmark

Install the library, then build and run inside generated9:

    cd /sys/src/libsqlite3/test
    mk bench
    ./6.bench :memory:
    ./6.bench -x :memory:

For file I/O, use a new database on a server satisfying sqlite3(2):

    rfork n
    ramfs -m /mnt
    ./6.bench /mnt/normal.db
    ./6.bench -x /mnt/exclusive.db

Existing files are refused; completed databases are left for inspection.
Default locking is NORMAL; -x selects EXCLUSIVE for this connection only.
mk test does not run the benchmark. mk clean removes its build products.

The workload has 100,000 rows, prepared inserts and primary-key lookups,
index creation, sorting, updates and five single-row commits per sync mode.
SQLite uses a 16 MiB page cache, 4096-byte pages and memory for temporary
tables. Journals use DELETE, or MEMORY for an in-memory database.
FULL sync is used until the final OFF comparison. OFF is diagnostic only.
Counts, checksums and integrity_check must pass before the final "ok".

Output columns are test, operation count, elapsed milliseconds, client CPU
milliseconds and checksum. Startup and printing are outside the timed regions.
Plan 9 CPU time has coarse resolution and excludes file-server processes.
Use separate database names for repeated runs and compare medians. Record
CPU, VM settings, SQLite version, cache settings and the storage path.
A gefs image served by u9fs and a virtio disk have very different I/O costs;
ramfs is volatile. Timing FULL sync does not certify power-loss durability.

Optional Linux comparison

bench.c also builds against the original amalgamation recorded in ../UPSTREAM.
From this directory, set src to the extracted upstream directory and match
the feature flags in ../plan9.h. For the current build:

    src=/path/to/sqlite-amalgamation-3540100
    cc -O2 -DNDEBUG -DSQLITE_THREADSAFE=0 \
        -DSQLITE_OMIT_LOAD_EXTENSION=1 -DSQLITE_OMIT_WAL=1 \
        -DSQLITE_OMIT_LOCALTIME=1 -DSQLITE_MAX_MMAP_SIZE=0 \
        -DSQLITE_POWERSAFE_OVERWRITE=0 -DSQLITE_DISABLE_INTRINSIC=1 \
        -DSQLITE_MAX_ALLOCATION_SIZE=0x3fffffff -I"$src" \
        bench.c "$src/sqlite3.c" -lm -o /tmp/sqlite-bench
    /tmp/sqlite-bench :memory:

Use the same workload and locking mode. An in-memory comparison includes
compiler, libc and operating-system differences, not just VM overhead.
